putation, and noteworthy claims.\n\n# Report Structure\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insights about the community. Each insight should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded according to the grounding rules below. Be comprehensive.\n\nReturn output as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\n# Example Input\n-----------\nText:\n\nEntities\n\nid,entity,description\n5,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n6,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is an organization that is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n\nRelationships\n\nid,source,target,description\n37,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n38,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n39,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,The Unity March is taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n40,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,TRIBUNE SPOTLIGHT,Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity march taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n41,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,BAILEY ASADI,Bailey Asadi is speaking at Verdant Oasis Plaza about the march\n43,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,UNITY MARCH,Harmony Assembly is organizing the Unity March\n\nOutput:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza and Unity March\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community revolves around the Verdant Oasis Plaza, which is the location of the Unity March. The plaza has relationships with the Harmony Assembly, Unity March, and Tribune Spotlight, all of which are associated with the march event.\",\n    \"rating\": 5.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the potential for unrest or conflict during the Unity March.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza as the central location\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza is the central entity in this community, serving as the location for the Unity March. This plaza is the common link between all other entities, suggesting its significance in the community. The plaza's association with the march could potentially lead to issues such as public disorder or conflict, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. [Data: Entities (5), Relationships (37, 38, 39, 40, 41,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Harmony Assembly's role in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Harmony Assembly is another key entity in this community, being the organizer of the march at Verdant Oasis Plaza. The nature of Harmony Assembly and its march could be a potential source of threat, depending on their objectives and the reactions they provoke. The relationship between Harmony Assembly and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Entities(6), Relationships (38, 43)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Unity March as a significant event\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Unity March is a significant event taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza. This event is a key factor in the community's dynamics and could be a potential source of threat, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. The relationship between the march and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Relationships (39)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Tribune Spotlight\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity March taking place in Verdant Oasis Plaza. This suggests that the event has attracted media attention, which could amplify its impact on the community. The role of Tribune Spotlight could be significant in shaping public perception of the event and the entities involved. [Data: Relationships (40)]\"\n        }\n    ]\n}\n\n\n# Real Data\n\nUse the following text for your answer. Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n1271,BATTERY DESIGN EXPERIMENT,\"### Comprehensive Summary of Battery Design Experiment\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nThe Battery Design Experiment is a structured study aimed at analyzing the effective life of batteries under varying temperature conditions and different material types. The primary objective is to understand how these factors influence battery performance, thereby guiding the selection of optimal materials and operating conditions for enhanced battery longevity.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**  \nIn the context of this experiment, a typical statistical model may be formulated as follows:\n\n\\[ Y_{ijk} = \\mu + \\alpha_i + \\beta_j + \\epsilon_{ijk} \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( Y_{ijk} \\) is the response variable (battery life),\n- \\( \\mu \\) is the overall mean,\n- \\( \\alpha_i \\) represents the effect of the \\( i \\)-th temperature condition,\n- \\( \\beta_j \\) represents the effect of the \\( j \\)-th material type,\n- \\( \\epsilon_{ijk} \\) is the random error term associated with the \\( k \\)-th observation under the \\( i \\)-th temperature and \\( j \\)-th material.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nThe design of the Battery Design Experiment can be structured as a factorial design, where each combination of temperature conditions and material types is tested. This allows for the examination of both main effects (individual impacts of temperature and material) and interaction effects (how the combination of temperature and material influences battery life).\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nThe following assumptions must be met for the validity of the analysis:\n1. Independence of observations: Each battery's performance is independent of others.\n2. Normality: The residuals of the model should be approximately normally distributed.\n3. Homogeneity of variance: The variance of battery life should be constant across all groups defined by temperature and material types.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nThe Battery Design Experiment is applicable when researchers aim to evaluate the effects of multiple factors on a continuous response variable, such as battery life. It is particularly useful when the factors are categorical (e.g., different materials and temperature levels) and when interactions between factors are of interest.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis typically involves conducting an Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) to determine if there are statistically significant differences in battery life across the different groups defined by the factors. Post-hoc tests may be employed to identify specific group differences if significant effects are found.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \n- The factorial design allows for the simultaneous examination of multiple factors and their interactions, providing a comprehensive understanding of the influences on battery life.\n- ANOVA is a robust statistical method that can handle multiple groups and is widely accepted in scientific research.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \n- The complexity of the design increases with the number of factors and levels, which may lead to challenges in data collection and analysis.\n- If assumptions of ANOVA are violated, the results may be misleading, necessitating the use of alternative methods or transformations.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRelated experimental designs include Response Surface Methodology (RSM) for optimizing battery performance and Taguchi methods for robust design. Alternatives to ANOVA include non-parametric tests (e.g., Kruskal-Wallis test) when assumptions of normality are not met.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nResearchers should ensure adequate sample sizes to achieve sufficient power for detecting effects. Randomization of experimental runs is crucial to mitigate bias. Additionally, careful calibration of measurement instruments is necessary to ensure accurate data collection.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nStatistical software such as R, SAS, or SPSS can be utilized to perform ANOVA and other related analyses. These tools provide functionalities for model fitting, assumption checking, and post-hoc testing.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nThe Battery Design Experiment is commonly applied in the fields of materials science and electrical engineering, particularly in the development and testing of new battery technologies. It serves as a practical example in educational settings to illustrate the application of ANOVA in experimental design.\",8\r\n266,TUKAY'S TEST,\"**Tukey's Test: Comprehensive Summary**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nTukey's Test, also known as Tukey's Honestly Significant Difference (HSD) test, is a multiple comparison method employed in the context of Analysis of Variance (ANOVA). Its primary purpose is to identify specific differences between group means following a significant ANOVA result. This test is particularly useful in Life Data analysis, where it helps to determine which material types exhibit statistically significant differences in their means.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation:**\nTukey's Test compares the means of different groups by calculating the Tukey HSD statistic. The formula for the Tukey HSD is given by:\n\n\\[\nHSD = q_{\\alpha, k, df} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{MSE}{n}}\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( q_{\\alpha, k, df} \\) is the critical value from the Studentized range distribution for a given significance level \\( \\alpha \\), number of groups \\( k \\), and degrees of freedom \\( df \\).\n- \\( MSE \\) is the mean square error from the ANOVA.\n- \\( n \\) is the number of observations in each group (assuming equal sample sizes).\n\n**Design Layout:**\nTukey's Test is applied after conducting a one-way ANOVA, which involves a single factor with multiple levels (groups). The design layout typically consists of:\n- One independent variable (factor) with multiple levels (groups).\n- Dependent variable measured for each group.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of Tukey's Test relies on several assumptions:\n1. Independence of observations: The samples must be independent of each other.\n2. Normality: The data in each group should be approximately normally distributed.\n3. Homogeneity of variances: The variances among the groups should be equal (homoscedasticity).\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nTukey's Test is applicable when:\n- A significant ANOVA result indicates that at least one group mean is different.\n- The researcher wishes to conduct pairwise comparisons among group means.\n- The assumptions of normality and homogeneity of variances are met.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis procedure for Tukey's Test involves the following steps:\n1. Conduct a one-way ANOVA to test for overall differences among group means.\n2. If the ANOVA is significant, apply Tukey's Test to perform pairwise comparisons between group means.\n3. Calculate the Tukey HSD and compare the differences between group means to the HSD value to determine which means are significantly different.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Tukey's Test controls the family-wise error rate, reducing the likelihood of Type I errors when making multiple comparisons.\n- It provides a straightforward method for identifying specific group differences after ANOVA.\n- The test is robust to violations of normality when sample sizes are equal.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- Tukey's Test assumes equal sample sizes; if the sample sizes are unequal, the test may not perform optimally.\n- It may be less powerful than other methods (e.g., Dunnett's test) when comparing a control group to multiple treatment groups.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\n- Other post-hoc tests include Bonferroni correction, Scheffé's method, and Dunnett's test, each with its own strengths and weaknesses.\n- Tukey's Test is often used in conjunction with one-way ANOVA but can also be applied in more complex designs, such as factorial ANOVA.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nResearchers should ensure that the assumptions of Tukey's Test are met before application. If assumptions are violated, alternative methods or transformations may be necessary. Additionally, the choice of significance level (e.g., 0.05) should be determined prior to analysis.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nTukey's Test can be implemented in various statistical software packages, including R (using the `TukeyHSD()` function), SAS (using the `Tukey` option in PROC GLM), and SPSS (using the post-hoc tests option in ANOVA procedures).\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nTukey's Test is commonly used in various fields, including agriculture, psychology, and biomedical research, where researchers need to compare the means of different treatment groups or conditions. For example, it may be used to compare the effectiveness of different fertilizers on crop yield or to assess the impact of different teaching methods on student performance.\",2\r\n1317,RSQUARE,\"**RSQUARE: Comprehensive Summary**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nRSquare, also known as the coefficient of determination, is a statistical measure that quantifies the proportion of variance in a dependent variable that can be explained by one or more independent variables in a regression model. It serves as an indicator of the goodness of fit of the model, providing insight into how well the independent variables account for the variability in the dependent variable.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation:**\nMathematically, RSquare is defined as:\n\n\\[\nR^2 = 1 - \\frac{SS_{res}}{SS_{tot}}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(SS_{res}\\) is the sum of squares of the residuals (the differences between observed and predicted values), and \\(SS_{tot}\\) is the total sum of squares (the variance of the dependent variable). The value of RSquare ranges from 0 to 1, where 0 indicates that the model explains none of the variability and 1 indicates that it explains all the variability.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nWhile RSquare itself does not have a design layout, it is typically used in the context of regression analysis, which may involve various experimental designs such as factorial designs, response surface designs, or observational studies where the relationship between dependent and independent variables is assessed.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of RSquare relies on several assumptions inherent to regression analysis:\n1. Linearity: The relationship between the independent and dependent variables is linear.\n2. Independence: Observations are independent of each other.\n3. Homoscedasticity: The variance of residuals is constant across all levels of the independent variables.\n4. Normality: The residuals of the model are normally distributed.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nRSquare is applicable in situations where the researcher aims to understand the explanatory power of independent variables in predicting a dependent variable. It is particularly useful in linear regression models and can be used to compare the fit of different models. However, it should not be the sole criterion for model selection, as it does not account for the number of predictors in the model.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nTo compute RSquare, one typically follows these steps:\n1. Fit a regression model to the data.\n2. Calculate the predicted values and the residuals.\n3. Compute \\(SS_{res}\\) and \\(SS_{tot}\\).\n4. Apply the RSquare formula to obtain the coefficient.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Provides a clear and interpretable measure of model fit.\n- Facilitates comparison between different regression models.\n- Helps in understanding the proportion of variance explained by the model.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- RSquare can be misleading, especially in models with many predictors, as it tends to increase with the addition of variables regardless of their relevance.\n- It does not indicate whether the independent variables are a cause of the changes in the dependent variable.\n- It does not provide information about the accuracy of predictions.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated concepts include Adjusted RSquare, which adjusts the RSquare value based on the number of predictors, and other model evaluation metrics such as AIC (Akaike Information Criterion) and BIC (Bayesian Information Criterion) that account for model complexity.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen using RSquare, researchers should ensure that the assumptions of regression analysis are met. It is also advisable to use Adjusted RSquare when comparing models with different numbers of predictors to avoid overfitting.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nRSquare can be easily computed using statistical software packages such as R, Python (with libraries like statsmodels and scikit-learn), SAS, and SPSS, which provide built-in functions for regression analysis and model evaluation.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nRSquare is commonly used in various fields, including economics, biology, engineering, and social sciences, to assess the effectiveness of predictive models. For example, it may be used to evaluate how well various factors explain the filtration rate in a chemical process or to assess the impact of marketing strategies on sales performance.\",6\r\n1228,GRAIN SIZE,Grain size is the outcome variable being measured in the experiments conducted by the aluminum master alloy manufacturer.,2\r\n539,DESIGN-EXPERT,\"**Design-Expert: Comprehensive Summary**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nDesign-Expert is a specialized software package designed to facilitate the design of experiments (DOE) and perform statistical analysis, particularly focusing on analysis of variance (ANOVA). It serves as a tool for experimenters to generate optimal experimental designs, fit statistical models to data, and analyze results across various fields, including engineering, manufacturing, and semiconductor processes.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure:**\nDesign-Expert employs various algorithms to generate D-optimal designs, which are statistical designs that maximize the determinant of the information matrix, thereby providing the most efficient parameter estimates. The software also supports regression modeling, particularly in the context of response surface methodology (RSM), where models are typically expressed as polynomial functions of the input variables.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nThe software offers a range of design options, including:\n- D-optimal designs for general experimental setups.\n- Two-level factorial and fractional factorial designs, which are structured to explore the effects of multiple factors efficiently.\n- Minimum-run resolution IV designs, which ensure that main effects can be estimated without confounding with other effects.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nFor the results obtained using Design-Expert to be valid, several assumptions must be met:\n- The experimental errors should be normally distributed.\n- The variances of the errors should be constant (homoscedasticity).\n- The relationship between the response and the factors should be adequately modeled by the chosen statistical model.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nDesign-Expert is applicable in scenarios where efficient experimental designs are required, particularly when:\n- The number of factors is large, and traditional designs would be impractical.\n- There is a need for optimization in response surface methodology.\n- The experimenter seeks to analyze factorial designs or perform robust parameter design.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis procedure in Design-Expert typically involves:\n1. Setting up the experimental design based on the factors and levels.\n2. Conducting the experiments and collecting data.\n3. Using the software to fit statistical models to the data.\n4. Performing ANOVA to assess the significance of factors and interactions.\n5. Conducting model adequacy checks and diagnostics.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Design-Expert provides a user-friendly interface that simplifies the process of experimental design and analysis.\n- It includes advanced algorithms for generating D-optimal designs, which can lead to more efficient experiments.\n- The software supports a wide range of designs and analysis methods, making it versatile for various applications.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- The effectiveness of the designs generated is contingent upon the correct specification of the model and assumptions.\n- Users may require training to fully utilize the software's capabilities, particularly for complex designs.\n- The cost of the software may be a barrier for some users or organizations.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nDesign-Expert is related to other statistical software packages that support DOE and ANOVA, such as Minitab, JMP, and R. Each of these alternatives has its strengths and may be preferred based on specific user needs or familiarity.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing Design-Expert, users should consider:\n- The specific requirements of their experimental setup, including the number of factors and levels.\n- The need for training or support to effectively use the software.\n- The integration of the software with existing data collection and analysis workflows.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nDesign-Expert is available as a standalone software package, and it is often referenced in academic literature and textbooks related to experimental design. It provides tools for Box-Cox transformations and other statistical analyses, enhancing its utility in various experimental contexts.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nDesign-Expert is commonly used in fields such as:\n- Engineering for process optimization.\n- Manufacturing for quality control and robust parameter design.\n- Pharmaceutical research for formulation studies.\n- Semiconductor manufacturing for process improvement and yield enhancement.\n\nIn summary, Design-Expert is a powerful tool for experimenters seeking to optimize their experimental designs and perform comprehensive statistical analyses, making it a valuable asset in the field of Design of Experiments and Analysis of Variance.\",23\r\n1443,FILTRATION RATE,\"**Filtration Rate: Comprehensive Summary**\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nFiltration Rate is a critical response variable in experimental designs, particularly in the context of analyzing the efficiency of filtration processes. It quantifies how quickly a fluid passes through a filter, serving as a key performance indicator in various manufacturing and industrial applications. The measurement of filtration rate is essential for optimizing processes, ensuring product quality, and enhancing operational efficiency.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**  \nWhile specific mathematical formulations may vary depending on the context, the filtration rate can generally be expressed as:  \n\\[ \\text{Filtration Rate} = \\frac{\\text{Volume of Filtrate}}{\\text{Time}} \\]  \nThis formula indicates the volume of fluid that successfully passes through the filter over a specified time period, providing a quantitative measure of filtration efficiency.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nIn experimental designs, particularly in the Pilot Plant Filtration Rate Experiment, the layout typically involves controlled conditions where various factors (e.g., filter type, fluid properties, pressure) are systematically varied to observe their effects on the filtration rate. A factorial design may be employed to assess multiple factors simultaneously, allowing for the evaluation of both main effects and interactions.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nThe validity of the filtration rate measurements relies on several assumptions, including:  \n1. The filtration process is steady-state during the measurement period.\n2. The fluid properties (e.g., viscosity, density) remain constant.\n3. The filter characteristics (e.g., pore size, material) do not change during the experiment.\n4. The system is free from external disturbances that could affect the flow rate.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nFiltration rate measurements are applicable in scenarios where the efficiency of filtration processes is critical, such as in water treatment, pharmaceuticals, food processing, and chemical manufacturing. Selection criteria for conducting experiments involving filtration rate include the need for process optimization, quality control, and the investigation of new filtration technologies.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis of filtration rate data typically involves statistical methods such as Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) to determine the significance of different factors affecting the filtration rate. The procedure includes:  \n1. Collecting data on filtration rates under various experimental conditions.\n2. Performing ANOVA to assess the impact of factors and their interactions.\n3. Interpreting results to identify significant influences on filtration efficiency.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \nThe measurement of filtration rate provides several advantages, including:  \n- Direct quantification of filtration efficiency.\n- Ability to identify optimal operating conditions.\n- Facilitation of comparative studies between different filtration methods or materials.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \nDespite its strengths, there are limitations associated with measuring filtration rate, such as:  \n- Sensitivity to variations in fluid properties and external conditions.\n- Potential for measurement errors if not conducted under controlled conditions.\n- Limited applicability if the filtration process is not well understood or characterized.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRelated experimental designs include Response Surface Methodology (RSM) for optimizing multiple factors simultaneously and Taguchi methods for robust design. Alternatives to direct filtration rate measurement may involve modeling approaches or simulations to predict filtration performance under varying conditions.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing filtration rate experiments, considerations include ensuring proper calibration of measurement equipment, maintaining consistent environmental conditions, and adequately training personnel to minimize variability in data collection.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nStatistical software packages such as R, SAS, or Minitab can be utilized for conducting ANOVA and other statistical analyses related to filtration rate experiments. These tools facilitate data management, analysis, and visualization of results.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nFiltration rate is commonly measured in applications such as water purification systems, industrial wastewater treatment, beverage production (e.g., beer and wine filtration), and pharmaceutical manufacturing processes where the clarity and purity of the final product are paramount.\",14\r\n1266,TEMPERATURE,\"**Entity: TEMPERATURE**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nTemperature is a critical factor in various experimental designs, representing the heat applied to a system, typically measured in degrees Celsius (°C) or Fahrenheit (°F). It serves as an independent variable that influences the outcome of experiments across multiple fields, including chemistry, materials science, and engineering. The purpose of studying temperature in experiments is to understand its effect on various dependent variables, such as impurity levels, tensile strength, viscosity, and yield of products.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure:**\nIn the context of statistical analysis, temperature can be incorporated into regression models or ANOVA frameworks. For example, in a simple linear regression model, the relationship between temperature (T) and a response variable (Y) can be expressed as:\n\\[ Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 T + \\epsilon \\]\nwhere \\( \\beta_0 \\) is the intercept, \\( \\beta_1 \\) is the coefficient representing the effect of temperature, and \\( \\epsilon \\) is the error term.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nTemperature can be tested at various levels in an experimental design, such as a factorial design where multiple factors are varied simultaneously. For instance, in a 2^k factorial design, temperature could be one of the k factors, with each factor having two levels (e.g., low and high temperature).\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of experiments involving temperature as a factor relies on several assumptions:\n1. Independence of observations.\n2. Normality of residuals.\n3. Homogeneity of variances (homoscedasticity).\n4. Linearity of the relationship between temperature and the response variable.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nTemperature should be considered as a factor in experiments where it is hypothesized to influence the outcome. It is particularly relevant in chemical processes, material testing, and any scenario where thermal conditions can affect physical or chemical properties. Selection criteria include the nature of the response variable, the range of temperatures to be tested, and the specific objectives of the study.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis of temperature effects typically involves ANOVA to assess the significance of temperature levels on the response variable. If multiple factors are involved, a factorial ANOVA can be employed. Post-hoc tests may be conducted to determine which specific temperature levels differ significantly.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Temperature is a controllable variable, allowing for systematic investigation of its effects.\n- It can provide insights into optimal conditions for processes, enhancing efficiency and product quality.\n- The use of temperature in experimental designs can lead to robust models that predict outcomes under varying conditions.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- The effects of temperature may interact with other factors, complicating the analysis.\n- Extreme temperatures can lead to non-linear effects or damage to materials, limiting the range of testing.\n- The need for precise temperature control and measurement can increase experimental complexity and costs.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated experimental designs include factorial designs, response surface methodology (RSM), and central composite designs, which can all incorporate temperature as a factor. Alternatives to direct temperature manipulation may include using thermal simulations or computational models to predict temperature effects.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing temperature as a factor in experiments, researchers must ensure accurate temperature measurement and control. This may involve using calibrated thermocouples or temperature sensors and maintaining consistent environmental conditions throughout the experiment.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nStatistical software such as R, SAS, or Minitab can be utilized for conducting ANOVA and regression analyses involving temperature as a factor. These tools provide functionalities for designing experiments, analyzing data, and visualizing results.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nTemperature is commonly studied in various applications, including:\n- Chemical processes where yield and purity are affected by reaction temperature.\n- Material science experiments assessing the tensile strength and warping of materials at different temperatures.\n- Battery performance testing at varying temperatures to evaluate efficiency and lifespan.\n- Polymer viscosity studies where temperature influences flow characteristics. \n\nIn summary, temperature is a fundamental variable in experimental design and analysis, with significant implications across multiple scientific disciplines. Its careful consideration and analysis can lead to valuable insights and advancements in research and industry practices.\",36\r\n2246,EXAMPLE 10.2,\"**Comprehensive Summary of \"\"EXAMPLE 10.2\"\"**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\n\"\"EXAMPLE 10.2\"\" refers to a specific case study involving a \\(2^3\\) factorial design, which is utilized in the context of regression analysis. The primary purpose of this example is to illustrate the application of factorial designs in experimental settings, particularly in chemical engineering, and to emphasize the significance of orthogonal designs in ensuring the validity and efficiency of the analysis.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure:**\nIn a \\(2^3\\) factorial design, there are three factors, each at two levels (typically coded as -1 and +1). The general model for the response variable \\(Y\\) can be expressed as:\n\\[\nY = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X_1 + \\beta_2 X_2 + \\beta_3 X_3 + \\beta_{12} X_1 X_2 + \\beta_{13} X_1 X_3 + \\beta_{23} X_2 X_3 + \\beta_{123} X_1 X_2 X_3 + \\epsilon\n\\]\nwhere \\(X_i\\) represents the levels of the factors, \\(\\beta_i\\) are the coefficients to be estimated, and \\(\\epsilon\\) is the error term.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nThe \\(2^3\\) factorial design consists of eight experimental runs, corresponding to all combinations of the three factors at their two levels. The layout can be represented in a tabular format, showing the factor combinations and the resulting response measurements.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of the \\(2^3\\) factorial design and the subsequent regression analysis relies on several key assumptions:\n1. Independence of observations.\n2. Normality of the residuals.\n3. Homogeneity of variance (constant variance across levels of the factors).\n4. Linearity of the relationship between the factors and the response.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nThis design is particularly applicable when the researcher aims to explore the effects of multiple factors simultaneously and when interactions between factors are of interest. It is suitable for situations where the number of factors is manageable (in this case, three) and where the experimental runs can be conducted efficiently.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis typically involves fitting the regression model to the collected data, assessing the significance of the main effects and interactions using ANOVA, and checking the assumptions of the model. The analysis may also include diagnostic checks for residuals to validate the model fit.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\nThe \\(2^3\\) factorial design offers several advantages:\n- It allows for the simultaneous investigation of multiple factors and their interactions.\n- The design is efficient in terms of the number of experimental runs required.\n- Orthogonal designs facilitate straightforward interpretation of main effects and interactions.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\nDespite its strengths, the \\(2^3\\) factorial design has limitations:\n- It may become impractical with a larger number of factors due to the exponential increase in the number of runs.\n- The assumption of linearity may not hold in all cases, potentially leading to misleading conclusions.\n- Interaction effects can complicate the analysis and interpretation if not properly accounted for.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated designs include fractional factorial designs, which allow for the study of a larger number of factors with fewer runs, and response surface methodology, which is used for optimization problems. Alternatives may also include completely randomized designs or block designs, depending on the experimental context.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing a \\(2^3\\) factorial design, researchers should consider the availability of resources, the feasibility of conducting all runs, and the potential need for randomization to mitigate bias. Proper planning for data collection and analysis is crucial to ensure robust results.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nStatistical software packages such as R, SAS, and Minitab provide tools for designing and analyzing factorial experiments, including the \\(2^3\\) design. These tools facilitate the fitting of regression models and the execution of ANOVA tests.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\n\"\"EXAMPLE 10.2\"\" is situated within the realm of chemical engineering, where factorial designs are frequently employed to optimize processes, such as reaction conditions, material properties, or product yields. This example serves as a reference point for understanding the practical application of factorial designs in real-world scenarios.\",8\r\n2335,FIRST-ORDER MODEL,\"### Comprehensive Summary of FIRST-ORDER MODEL\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nA first-order model is a linear statistical representation used in experimental design to approximate the relationship between one or more independent variables and a dependent variable. It serves as a foundational model in various contexts, including inventory management and crystal growth, and is often employed as a baseline for more complex models.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation**  \nThe first-order model can be mathematically expressed as:  \n\\[ Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X_1 + \\beta_2 X_2 + \\ldots + \\beta_k X_k + \\epsilon \\]  \nwhere \\( Y \\) is the dependent variable, \\( \\beta_0 \\) is the intercept, \\( \\beta_1, \\beta_2, \\ldots, \\beta_k \\) are the coefficients for the independent variables \\( X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k \\), and \\( \\epsilon \\) represents the error term. This model includes only linear terms and does not account for higher-order interactions or polynomial terms.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nIn experimental designs utilizing a first-order model, the layout typically involves a systematic variation of the independent variables across a defined range. This can be structured as a full factorial design, where all combinations of factor levels are tested, or as a fractional factorial design, which tests a subset of combinations to reduce the number of experimental runs.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nThe validity of a first-order model relies on several key assumptions:\n1. Linearity: The relationship between the independent and dependent variables is linear.\n2. Independence: Observations are independent of one another.\n3. Homoscedasticity: The variance of the errors is constant across all levels of the independent variables.\n4. Normality: The residuals (errors) are normally distributed.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nA first-order model is applicable when the relationship between the variables is expected to be linear and when the complexity of the system does not warrant higher-order models. It is particularly useful in preliminary studies to identify trends and relationships before exploring more complex interactions.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis of a first-order model typically involves:\n1. Fitting the model to the data using least squares estimation.\n2. Evaluating the goodness-of-fit through R-squared values and residual analysis.\n3. Conducting hypothesis tests on the coefficients to determine their significance.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \nThe first-order model offers several advantages:\n- Simplicity: It is easy to understand and interpret.\n- Computational efficiency: It requires less computational power compared to higher-order models.\n- Quick identification of linear relationships: It allows for rapid assessment of the effects of independent variables on the dependent variable.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \nDespite its strengths, the first-order model has limitations:\n- Inability to capture non-linear relationships: It may provide a poor fit if the true relationship is non-linear.\n- Oversimplification: Important interactions or higher-order effects may be overlooked, leading to incomplete conclusions.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRelated methods include higher-order polynomial models, interaction models, and response surface methodology (RSM) that incorporate quadratic and interaction terms to better capture the complexities of the data.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing a first-order model, researchers should ensure that the assumptions are met and consider conducting residual analysis to validate the model. It is also important to choose an appropriate range for the independent variables to ensure that the linear approximation is valid.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nStatistical software packages such as R, SAS, and Minitab can be used to fit first-order models, perform regression analysis, and conduct hypothesis testing on the model coefficients.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nFirst-order models are commonly used in various fields, including:\n- Industrial experiments to optimize processes.\n- Agricultural studies to assess the impact of fertilizers on crop yield.\n- Quality control in manufacturing to evaluate the effects of different production parameters on product quality.\n\nIn summary, the first-order model is a fundamental tool in the design of experiments and statistical analysis, providing a straightforward approach to understanding linear relationships between variables while serving as a stepping stone to more complex modeling techniques.\",11\r\n982,FISHER LSD,\"**Fisher's Least Significant Difference (LSD)**\n\nFisher's Least Significant Difference (LSD) is a statistical procedure employed in the context of multiple comparisons to ascertain which treatment means differ significantly from one another. It is particularly useful following an Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) when the null hypothesis is rejected, indicating that at least one treatment mean is different from the others.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure:**\nThe LSD method is based on the t-distribution. The formula for calculating the LSD is given by:\n\n\\[\nLSD = t_{\\alpha/2, df} \\times \\sqrt{\\frac{2 \\times MSE}{n}}\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( t_{\\alpha/2, df} \\) is the critical value from the t-distribution for a specified significance level \\(\\alpha\\) and degrees of freedom \\(df\\),\n- \\( MSE \\) is the mean square error from the ANOVA,\n- \\( n \\) is the number of observations per treatment group.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nFisher's LSD is typically applied after a completely randomized design or a randomized block design where the ANOVA has indicated significant differences among treatment means. The design structure involves multiple treatment groups, each receiving a different level of the factor under investigation.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of Fisher's LSD relies on several key assumptions:\n1. The samples are independent.\n2. The data within each group are normally distributed.\n3. The variances among the groups are equal (homogeneity of variance).\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nFisher's LSD is applicable when:\n- The ANOVA results indicate significant differences among treatment means.\n- The number of treatment groups is relatively small, as LSD can lead to increased Type I error rates with larger numbers of comparisons.\n- The researcher seeks to identify specific group differences rather than just overall significance.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis procedure involves the following steps:\n1. Conduct an ANOVA to test for overall differences among treatment means.\n2. If the ANOVA is significant, calculate the LSD value.\n3. Compare the absolute differences between each pair of treatment means to the LSD value to determine which means are significantly different.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Fisher's LSD is straightforward to compute and interpret.\n- It provides a clear method for identifying specific differences between treatment means.\n- It is particularly effective when the number of treatments is small.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- The method can lead to an increased risk of Type I errors when many comparisons are made, as it does not adjust for multiple testing.\n- It assumes equal variances, which may not hold in practice, potentially leading to misleading results.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nAlternatives to Fisher's LSD include Tukey's Honestly Significant Difference (HSD) test, Bonferroni correction, and Scheffé's method, which provide more conservative approaches to multiple comparisons and control for Type I error rates.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing Fisher's LSD, researchers should ensure that the assumptions of normality and homogeneity of variance are met. If these assumptions are violated, alternative methods should be considered. Additionally, the number of comparisons should be limited to avoid inflated Type I error rates.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nFisher's LSD can be easily implemented in statistical software packages such as R, SAS, SPSS, and Python, which provide built-in functions for conducting ANOVA and subsequent post-hoc tests.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nFisher's LSD is commonly used in agricultural experiments, clinical trials, and any field where researchers need to compare the effects of different treatments or conditions on a response variable. For instance, it may be applied to determine which fertilizer type leads to the highest crop yield among several tested options.\",2\r\n91,COST,\"**Entity: COST**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nCost is a critical factor in the Design of Experiments (DOE) and Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) that pertains to the financial resources required to conduct experiments. It plays a significant role in the optimization process, where the objective is to minimize excessive costs while achieving reliable and valid experimental results. Understanding and managing costs is essential for ensuring that experiments are feasible and sustainable.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure:**\nWhile cost itself may not have a direct mathematical formulation, it can be integrated into optimization models where cost functions are minimized. For instance, in a factorial experiment, the total cost can be expressed as a function of the number of runs, materials, and labor involved, which can be represented as:\n\\[ C = f(n, m, l) \\]\nwhere \\( C \\) is the total cost, \\( n \\) is the number of experimental runs, \\( m \\) is the cost of materials, and \\( l \\) is the labor cost.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nIn the context of experimental design, cost considerations can influence the layout of factorial experiments. For example, a full factorial design may be too costly, leading researchers to opt for a fractional factorial design, which reduces the number of experimental runs while still providing valuable information about the factors being studied.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\n1. The cost estimates must be accurate and reflect the true financial resources required.\n2. The relationship between cost and experimental factors should be linear or appropriately modeled.\n3. The experimental design should be robust enough to account for variations in cost without compromising the integrity of the results.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nCost considerations are particularly relevant when:\n- Budget constraints exist, necessitating careful planning of experimental designs.\n- The scale of the experiment is large, making full factorial designs impractical.\n- The researcher aims to balance cost with the need for statistical power and precision.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nWhile cost itself is not analyzed in the same way as experimental factors, it can be included in the analysis of variance to assess the impact of different factors on overall costs. Cost data can be analyzed using regression techniques to identify significant cost drivers.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Incorporating cost into experimental design allows for more efficient use of resources.\n- It enables researchers to conduct experiments that are financially viable and sustainable.\n- Cost analysis can lead to better decision-making regarding the allocation of resources.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- Focusing too heavily on cost may lead to compromises in experimental quality or design.\n- Cost estimates can be uncertain, leading to potential inaccuracies in planning and execution.\n- There may be a trade-off between cost and the comprehensiveness of the experimental design.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\n- Fractional factorial designs are often used as a cost-effective alternative to full factorial designs.\n- Response surface methodology (RSM) can also be employed to optimize processes while considering cost constraints.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nResearchers should conduct a thorough cost-benefit analysis before finalizing their experimental design. This includes estimating all potential costs associated with materials, labor, and overhead, and considering the implications of these costs on the overall research objectives.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nVarious statistical software packages, such as R, SAS, and Minitab, can assist in modeling costs and analyzing experimental designs. These tools often include features for cost analysis and optimization.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nCost considerations are prevalent in fields such as manufacturing, pharmaceuticals, and agriculture, where experiments must be designed to optimize processes while minimizing financial expenditure. For instance, in a manufacturing setting, a company may conduct a fractional factorial experiment to determine the optimal settings for a production process while keeping costs within budget.\",2\r\n1334,COPPER PLATES,An experiment conducted to investigate the warping of copper plates under varying temperatures and copper content.,3\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n1560,BATTERY DESIGN EXPERIMENT,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,The Battery Design Experiment utilizes Analysis of Variance to analyze the data collected from the experiment,270\r\n250,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,TUKAY'S TEST,Tukey's test is a specific type of multiple comparison test used following ANOVA to determine which means are significantly different.,264\r\n1671,RSQUARE,ANOVA,RSquare is often reported in ANOVA results to indicate how well the model explains the variability of the response variable.,169\r\n1506,GRAIN SIZE,EXPERIMENT,The experiment aims to analyze the effect of stirring rate on the grain size,143\r\n602,DESIGN-EXPERT,EXPERIMENTAL DESIGN,Design-Expert is a software tool used in the context of experimental design,56\r\n1900,FILTRATION RATE,TEMPERATURE,\"### Comprehensive Summary of Filtration Rate and Temperature in Experimental Design\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nFiltration Rate refers to the speed at which a fluid passes through a filter, which is a critical parameter in various industrial and laboratory processes. Temperature is a controllable factor that significantly influences the filtration rate, as variations in temperature can alter the viscosity of the fluid, the solubility of solutes, and the overall efficiency of the filtration process. Understanding the relationship between temperature and filtration rate is essential for optimizing filtration systems and improving process efficiency.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation:**\nThe relationship between Filtration Rate (FR) and Temperature (T) can often be modeled using a linear regression equation or a more complex polynomial model, depending on the nature of the relationship. A simple linear model can be expressed as:\n\\[ FR = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 T + \\epsilon \\]\nwhere \\( \\beta_0 \\) is the intercept, \\( \\beta_1 \\) is the coefficient representing the effect of temperature on filtration rate, and \\( \\epsilon \\) is the error term.\n\n**Design Layout:**\nIn an experimental design to study the effect of temperature on filtration rate, a factorial design could be employed. This design would involve varying temperature levels (e.g., low, medium, high) while measuring the corresponding filtration rates. The layout would include multiple replications at each temperature level to ensure statistical validity.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\n1. Independence: Observations of filtration rates at different temperature levels should be independent.\n2. Normality: The distribution of the residuals should be approximately normal.\n3. Homoscedasticity: The variance of filtration rates should be constant across different temperature levels.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nThis experimental design is applicable when the researcher aims to understand how temperature affects filtration rate. It is particularly useful in scenarios where temperature can be easily controlled and varied, such as in laboratory settings or industrial processes.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis of the data collected from the experiment can be performed using Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) to determine if there are statistically significant differences in filtration rates across different temperature levels. If significant differences are found, post-hoc tests can be conducted to identify which temperature levels differ from each other.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Provides a clear understanding of the relationship between temperature and filtration rate.\n- Allows for the identification of optimal temperature settings for maximum filtration efficiency.\n- Facilitates the exploration of interactions if multiple factors are included in the design.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- The results may be influenced by uncontrolled variables that affect filtration rate.\n- The model may not capture non-linear relationships unless a more complex model is used.\n- Requires careful control of temperature to ensure valid results.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated designs include Response Surface Methodology (RSM) for exploring the optimal conditions when multiple factors are involved. Alternatives may include using a completely randomized design if the temperature levels are not expected to interact with other factors.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing this experimental design, it is crucial to ensure accurate temperature control and measurement. Calibration of equipment and proper randomization of trials can help mitigate biases. Additionally, sufficient replication is necessary to achieve reliable statistical power.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nStatistical software such as R, SAS, or Minitab can be utilized for data analysis, including ANOVA and regression modeling. These tools provide functionalities for visualizing results and conducting post-hoc analyses.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nCommon applications of studying the relationship between temperature and filtration rate include water treatment processes, pharmaceutical manufacturing, and food processing, where maintaining optimal filtration efficiency is critical for product quality and operational efficiency.\",50\r\n3055,EXAMPLE 10.2,TEMPERATURE,Temperature is one of the process variables analyzed in Example 10.2,44\r\n3204,FIRST-ORDER MODEL,EXPERIMENTAL DESIGN,The first-order model is fitted based on the data collected from the experimental design,44\r\n1148,PLASMA ETCHING EXPERIMENT,FISHER LSD,Fisher's LSD can be used in the plasma etching experiment to compare means and determine significant differences.,43\r\n92,FACTORIAL EXPERIMENT,COST,\"Cost considerations can affect the design and scale of factorial experiments, influencing the number of plots and factors used.\",40\r\n1710,COPPER PLATES,TEMPERATURE,The experiment investigates how different temperatures affect the warping of copper plates,39\r\n\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insights about the community. Each insight should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded according to the grounding rules below. Be comprehensive.\n\nReturn output as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\nOutput:", "role": "user"}], "parameters": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "frequency_penalty": 0.0, "max_tokens": 4000, "n": 1, "presence_penalty": 0.0, "temperature": 0.0, "top_p": 1.0, "response_format": {"type": "json_object"}}}, "key": "chat_create_community_report_9c6e87aac58ec5dee0417d9759f590640f7a507b395facf0066f2bebd002c3a9_v2"}